      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="chk_matched">
        <property name="checked">
         <bool>true</bool>
        </property>
        <property name="text">
         <string>Matched filter (weigh each pixel by the star's shape)</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>WHAT IT IS: instead of adding up the pixels inside a circle, each pixel is weighed by how much light the star's shape says it should hold, and the total is divided by the sum of those weights. With a known shape and white noise that is the best linear estimator there is, so it is not a trick, it is the arithmetic optimum. It is applied to the target AND to every comparison star, because the zero point has to come from the same arithmetic as the target.

WHAT IT PRODUCES, measured on real 2025 UR data: 1.55 to 1.63 times the aperture's signal-to-noise on a 139-frame stack; the zero point's error 2.6 times smaller (0.035 against 0.092 magnitudes); and the brightness bias at low signal-to-noise halved (from +0.54 to +0.23 magnitudes at SNR 9). It costs 3 % more time. The shape comes from the seeing measured on the plate, so nothing extra is asked of you.

THE RISKS, said plainly: it moves the magnitude that gets published, so a curve built before this will show a step of around 0.05 to 0.15 magnitudes (towards the truth, not away from it); and its flux follows the shape of the star, so where that shape changes across the field (a visit with two runs, a field with coma) it can move by 0.1 magnitudes from star to star, which the zero point spreads out but does not erase. Both were measured against a real catalogue (40 Gaia stars on a real visit): the zero point's error is 0.012 magnitudes against the aperture's 0.010, and against the catalogue the aperture UNDERESTIMATES the faint stars by 0.037 magnitudes where the filter is off by 0.007. It has not been checked on host-subtracted images, nor with a trailed object. The aperture's value is kept beside the reported one either way, so you can always compare. Untick it to go back to the aperture: the proven path</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
